                "text": "I'm testing a hypothesis about diagonal staircases of blocks being extended by one block on each end, colored 8. Checking the first training example, the placement doesn't match a simple extension\u2014the 8s appear at (2,6) and (5,9) rather than the expected (2,9) and (5,6), so I'm cross-checking against the third training example to see if there's a mirroring rule instead.\n\nFor train3, the blocks go down-right in steps of 2, but the extension appears reflected horizontally\u2014up-right and down-left instead of continuing straight. Train2 confirms this reflection: the natural extension direction is flipped, and the block landing exactly matches when I account for the boundary clipping at row9.\n\nGoing back to train1 with single-cell blocks, I'm trying to work out the exact offset transformation\u2014testing whether the step vector between blocks gets flipped in sign on one axis, but my calculations for (3,8)\u2192(4,7) aren't lining up with the expected (5,9) placement, so I need to reconsider whether the flip is per-block or reflects across the whole diagonal's midpoint.\n\nConfirming the pattern: horizontal offset doubles the width in the direction mirrored from the original diagonal step, while vertical offset stays equal to height. So extending up gives up-left when original moved down-left, extending down gives down-right - both consistent. I still need the actual test input to apply this rule.\n\n",
